.TH EUCLID-CLI-ENS-SET-TOPIC-DELETE-AFTER-DELIVERY 1 "October 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.73" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ens-set-topic-delete-after-delivery \- set whether a topic deletes a message once every subscriber has it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ens set-topic-delete-after-delivery
.BR \-\-topic | \-t
.I NAME
.BR \-\-enabled | \-e
.I true|false
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets whether a topic deletes each message as soon as it has been handed
to every subscription, instead of keeping it for the topic's retention
period.
.PP
Off for every topic until it is turned on here. That is how topics have
always behaved, and how a topic created before this setting existed
reads back: a message is kept for the retention period set with
.BR euclid-cli-ens-set-topic-retention (1),
and removed when that runs out.
.SH WHAT "HANDED TO" MEANS
A publish fans a message out to the topic's subscriptions at once: one
event per subscription, written to the event bus, which keeps it until
EQS has turned it into a queue message. With this setting on, the topic
message is deleted right after that \- the queue messages are the copies
that remain.
.PP
A message published while the topic is stopped
.RB ( euclid-cli-ens-stop-topic (1))
is held, not handed on, and is deleted when
.BR euclid-cli-ens-start-topic (1)
delivers it.
.PP
A message on a topic with no subscription at all has been handed to
nobody, and is kept for the retention period as on any topic rather than
lost undelivered.
.SH WHAT IT COSTS
The topic's own record of what was published.
.BR euclid-cli-ens-resend-messages (1)
works from that record, so a message this deletes cannot be sent again:
a subscriber that was down, was subscribed after the fact, or failed
to process what it received has no way back to it.
.SH WHEN IT TAKES EFFECT
For messages published afterwards. Messages the topic already holds were
delivered under the old rule and stay until their retention runs out \-
turning this on is not a purge. To empty a topic, use
.BR euclid-cli-ens-purge-topic (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-topic " " \fINAME\fR
Topic name. A full ERN also works, and is what reaches another
namespace. Required.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-enabled " " \fItrue|false\fR
\fItrue\fR to delete each message once every subscription has it,
\fIfalse\fR to keep it for the retention period. Also accepts \fI1\fR,
\fI0\fR, \fIyes\fR, \fIno\fR, \fIon\fR and \fIoff\fR. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The setting was changed; the topic's ERN and its new setting are
printed.
.TP
.B 1
No topic of that name, or a value that is not a yes or a no.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# A topic whose subscribers are the only record anybody needs
euclid-cli ens set-topic-delete-after-delivery --topic katalogdaten-result-topic --enabled true

# Back to keeping messages for the retention period
euclid-cli ens set-topic-delete-after-delivery --topic katalogdaten-result-topic --enabled false
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ens (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-set-topic-retention (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ens-resend-messages (1),
